{"text": "XOM still has balance-sheet room, but the cushion has narrowed over the last year. Net debt to EBITDA moved from -1.02× [f_bdc39160b429@2024-06-30] to 0.44× [f_bdc39160b429@2026-03-31], while EBIT interest coverage fell from 50.20× [f_26daab1268b4@2024-06-30] to 23.64× [f_26daab1268b4@2026-03-31]. FCF to debt dropped from 65.3% [f_07d06fec6f66@2024-06-30] to 15.4% [f_07d06fec6f66@2026-03-31]. On a four-quarter basis, the latest four reads are still comfortably positive, but all three measures have clearly softened versus the same four quarters a year earlier [chart: net_debt_to_ebitda_xom] [chart: ebit_interest_coverage_xom] [chart: fcf_to_debt_xom].

On the filing side, the 10-K Item 7 says future debt levels could differ materially because of “global or regional changes or imbalances in the supply and demand for oil, natural gas, petrochemicals, and feedstocks and other market factors” and “the ability to access debt markets on favorable terms or at all” [f_92b2ec83adae]. It also flags “changes in interest and exchange rates” and “variable impacts of trading activities on our margins and results each quarter” [f_92b2ec83adae]. I did not get a quoted passage yet that specifically lists debt maturities or floating-rate exposure, so I cannot claim those details from the 10-K on the evidence in hand.

For the book context, XOM is 4.61% [f_b34532e61674] of port_001. The only beta the desk returned was XOM’s beta to SPY, -0.56× [f_75b6cef816bc]; it did not provide a beta to USO. So the best supported read is: XOM is a modest book weight, and its own equity beta is not showing a strong positive equity-market sensitivity, but I cannot translate that into a direct USO oil beta from the desk’s evidence. What this means for the question is that if oil falls, the company still has some room, but the recent compression in cash generation and coverage means the book should not treat the balance sheet as infinitely insulated. The weight is meaningful but not dominant, so a severe oil drawdown would matter more through earnings and cash-flow pressure than through outright leverage strain at the current numbers."}

{"text": "Apple says its biggest business risks are product and service quality failures, cybersecurity and data-security incidents, and risks from AI-enabled attacks. In the filing, Apple says its products and services can be affected by “design and manufacturing defects” and that “quality problems can adversely affect” user experience, reputation, demand, new product introductions, and sales [f_ba1c87d4d708]. It also says “losses or unauthorized access to or releases of confidential information” could create “significant reputational, financial, legal and operational consequences” [f_ba1c87d4d708]. And it says “The rapid advancement and widespread dissemination of artificial intelligence technologies significantly increases the risks associated with cyberattacks” [f_ba1c87d4d708].

On concentration, I do not yet have the actual product-revenue or geographic-revenue figures from the desk. What I do have is the desk’s own statement that those figures are “not held as figures; stated in Item 7 and the product table — read_filings” for product revenue and “not held as figures; stated in the segment note — read_filings” for geographic revenue, and that customer concentration is “not held as figures as figures; stated in Item 1 / the concentration note — read_filings” [f_7d640b3c949f]. The same desk note also says backlog is “not held as figures; stated in Item 7 where the issuer discloses it — read_filings” [f_7d640b3c949f].

For the book, Apple is held. The desk shows AAPL at 15.2% [f_15f5bdbebd48] of issuer concentration and $1.63M [f_8faad5d6b56e] market value. The desk also shows the AAPL issuer-concentration warning level at 15.0% [f_c23b9402a828], so the holding is sitting right at the warning threshold. The desk does not hold Apple’s product revenue, geographic revenue, backlog, or customer concentration as figures; it will only quote those from the filings."}

